---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "How to Choose a Dehumidifier",
    "url": "/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Whole House Dehumidifiers",
    "url": "/best-whole-house-dehumidifier/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifiers for Bedrooms",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifier for Basement",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Much Electricity Does a Dehumidifier Use?",
    "url": "/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Whole House vs Portable Dehumidifier",
    "url": "/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-30", modified: "2026-10-30" });
const title = "7 Best Dehumidifiers for the Home in 2026: Sized by Pint Rating";
const description =
  "Seven dehumidifiers for ordinary homes, picked by capacity under the 2019 DOE test, drainage and controls. Choose between 22, 35 and 50 pints.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "frigidaire-ffad3533w1-35-pint",
    "name": "Frigidaire FFAD3533W1 35-Pint Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Compressor, 35-Pint, Main Floor",
    "badge": "Best Overall for a Typical Home",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "35 pints is the middle capacity under the current DOE test, and the size that suits most ordinary houses",
      "Built-in hose connection for continuous gravity drainage, plus a removable bucket for occasional use",
      "Electronic humidistat lets you hold a target like 45% instead of running until the bucket fills",
      "Washable filter and a straightforward control panel with no app to set up",
      "Frigidaire sells through major retailers and parts and service are easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Compressor design loses efficiency in cool rooms, so it is a poor fit for an unheated space in winter",
      "Gravity drainage only: it needs to sit above a drain, so there is no built-in pump"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The default choice for most readers. It is the size most houses need, with the drainage and humidistat features that decide whether you actually use it.",
    "description": "If you want one recommendation without reading the rest of this guide, this is it. The Frigidaire FFAD3533W1 is rated at 35 pints, the middle of the three standard capacities under the 2019 DOE test, which is where an ordinary home with a stale-smelling main floor, a laundry room or a finished lower level usually lands. Frigidaire specifies a washable filter, an electronic humidistat and a hose outlet for continuous drainage, which together cover the three features that matter most in daily use: it can hold a humidity target, it can run for days without emptying, and it is easy to clean. We have no measured data of our own on it, so the case for it rests on specifications and on the pattern of owner reports, which tend to praise the straightforward controls and value for the capacity. The honest limits are the ones every compressor unit shares: it works best in rooms that stay reasonably warm, and it drains by gravity rather than by pump. Check the listing for the current Energy Star status and warranty terms before you buy."
  },
  {
    "id": "midea-cube-35-pint",
    "name": "Midea Cube 35-Pint Smart Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Compressor, 35-Pint, App Control",
    "badge": "Best Smart Controls",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Cube-shaped body with a built-in handle is easier to carry between rooms than a typical tall tower",
      "Wi-Fi and app control, with voice assistant support, lets you adjust it without walking to the room",
      "Designed to take a drain hose from the back for continuous drainage",
      "Humidity target and fan speed are adjustable, so you can trade drying speed for noise",
      "Available in several capacities, so the same layout is easy to scale up or down"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "App features add a setup step and depend on your home network behaving",
      "Usually priced above basic 35-pint units, and the smart features only matter if you will use them"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth the premium if you move your dehumidifier around or want to check humidity from your phone. If you set it once and leave it, the basic Frigidaire does the same drying for less.",
    "description": "The Midea Cube earns a place here mostly for its shape and its controls. The squared-off body, with a handle built into the top, makes it noticeably easier to relocate than a tall tower, which matters if you shift it from the laundry room to the hall after a muggy week. Midea specifies Wi-Fi and app control with voice assistant compatibility, so you can read the room humidity, change the target and switch it off without a trip downstairs. It is a compressor unit rated at 35 pints on the current DOE test, so the drying capacity sits level with the Frigidaire above; the difference is convenience rather than power. A rear hose port supports continuous drainage, which is the feature that stops a dehumidifier becoming a daily chore. The trade-offs are the usual ones for connected appliances: the app is one more thing that can need a firmware update or a Wi-Fi password change, and you pay extra for features you may never touch. If you will genuinely use remote control, it is the best of the 35-pint group; if not, save the money."
  },
  {
    "id": "homelabs-50-pint-dehumidifier",
    "name": "hOmeLabs 50-Pint Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Compressor, 50-Pint, Large or Damp Spaces",
    "badge": "Best for Large or Very Damp Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "50 pints is the top standard capacity, aimed at large open areas and genuinely damp conditions",
      "Hose connection for continuous drainage and a larger bucket than smaller models",
      "Digital humidistat with a selectable target and an auto-restart after a power cut",
      "Marketed for spaces of several thousand square feet, which suits open-plan lower levels",
      "Typically costs less than name-brand 50-pint competitors"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Bigger and louder than a 35-pint machine, and overkill for a single small room",
      "A less established service network than the large appliance brands, so read the warranty terms"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it when the space is large and truly damp. If your main floor is only mildly humid, a 35-pint unit will do the job more quietly.",
    "description": "Bigger is not always better with dehumidifiers, but sometimes the house really is that damp. The hOmeLabs 50-Pint is sold for large spaces, with the manufacturer marketing it for several thousand square feet, and the 50-pint rating is the top of the standard DOE capacity ladder. It suits an open-plan lower level, a house with high humidity for most of the summer, or a family that dries a lot of laundry indoors. As with the other compressor models here, it offers continuous drainage through a hose and a digital humidistat to hold a target, and an auto-restart feature so it resumes after a power cut instead of sitting idle. The trade-offs are size and sound: a 50-pint machine moves more air and draws more power than a 35, and in a small room it will cycle off quickly and can feel excessive. Our caution is about the brand rather than the machine. Check the warranty and the seller carefully, because support from a smaller appliance brand is not the same as support from a major one."
  },
  {
    "id": "honeywell-tp50awkn-50-pint",
    "name": "Honeywell TP50AWKN 50-Pint Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Compressor, 50-Pint, Established Brand",
    "badge": "Best 50-Pint From a Major Brand",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "50-pint capacity for big open areas, from a brand most buyers recognise",
      "Washable filter and a straightforward bucket design that is simple to maintain",
      "Hose connection for continuous drainage when you do not want to empty the bucket",
      "Digital humidity control with adjustable fan speeds",
      "Broadly available at big retailers, so returns and replacement parts are manageable"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Large and heavy when the bucket is full, and not something you will want to move often",
      "No built-in pump, so drainage still depends on gravity or on emptying the bucket"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A sensible alternative to the hOmeLabs if you prefer a larger, better-known brand behind a 50-pint machine.",
    "description": "The Honeywell TP50AWKN sits in the same capacity class as the hOmeLabs, and the argument for choosing it is brand and distribution. Honeywell specifies 50 pints, a washable filter, a digital humidity setting and a drain-hose outlet, which is the same feature set you should expect at this size. What you are paying for is mostly familiarity: it is sold through large retailers, which tends to make returns, replacements and warranty claims simpler than they are with a smaller marketplace brand. Owner reports commonly describe it as a workable, unfussy machine for a big damp area, and they also describe the usual limitations, namely noise at the highest fan speed and weight once the bucket is full. If you plan to drain by hose, that second complaint largely disappears. If your house is only moderately humid, step down to a 35-pint model, because a machine this large will reach its target and shut off often, which is not harmful but does mean you bought capacity you did not use. Check the current listing for exact dimensions and Energy Star status."
  },
  {
    "id": "frigidaire-ffad2233w1-22-pint",
    "name": "Frigidaire FFAD2233W1 22-Pint Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Compressor, 22-Pint, Smaller Homes",
    "badge": "Best for Apartments and Small Homes",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "22 pints is the smallest standard DOE capacity, which suits apartments and moderately damp small homes",
      "More compact and lighter than the larger models, so it moves easily between rooms",
      "Continuous drain option plus a removable bucket",
      "Typically draws less power than a 35- or 50-pint compressor machine",
      "Washable filter and simple controls"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Struggles with a genuinely wet or large space; do not expect it to dry a whole open-plan level",
      "Like any compressor unit, it slows down in cool rooms"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Right if your space is small and only mildly humid. If you are unsure, step up a size: an undersized unit runs constantly and still disappoints.",
    "description": "Most buyers should size up rather than down, but the Frigidaire FFAD2233W1 is the right call for an apartment, a small house with one troublesome room or a laundry nook that only gets sticky in the humid months. It is rated at 22 pints under the current DOE test, which is the smallest of the standard capacities, and it is lighter and more compact than the 35- and 50-pint machines, so it is easy to carry from room to room. Frigidaire lists the same practical features as its bigger sibling: a washable filter, an electronic humidity control and a hose outlet for continuous drainage. The point of buying small is lower power draw and a smaller footprint, and for the right space that is a fair trade. The risk is buying too little. A machine that is too small runs flat out, never reaches the target, and gets blamed for failing when it was simply the wrong size. Use the sizing section below, and if your reading is borderline between 22 and 35, choose 35."
  },
  {
    "id": "pro-breeze-mini-dehumidifier",
    "name": "Pro Breeze Mini Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Thermoelectric Mini, Closets and Small Rooms",
    "badge": "Best Mini for Closets and Bathrooms",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Small enough for a closet, bathroom, pantry or boat cabin",
      "Thermoelectric (Peltier) design has no compressor, so it is quiet and has no moving refrigerant parts",
      "Very low power draw compared with a full-size dehumidifier",
      "Auto shut-off when the tank is full",
      "Inexpensive enough to treat as a seasonal accessory"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Removes only a small volume of water per day and is weak against genuine room-wide dampness",
      "Thermoelectric units lose output in cooler, drier air, so expect little in a cold space"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A good fit for a closet or a bathroom corner. Do not mistake it for a way to dry a room.",
    "description": "A mini dehumidifier is a different tool from the rest of this list, and it is worth being clear about what it does. The Pro Breeze uses a thermoelectric, or Peltier, cooling plate rather than a compressor, which means it is silent apart from a small fan, draws very little power and is small enough to sit on a shelf. It suits the spots a full-size machine cannot reach: a closet where shoes smell musty, a pantry that is always a little sticky, a bathroom corner or a boat cabin. The tank is small, and the manufacturer positions it for small enclosed spaces, so you should expect to empty it regularly and to see modest results. Owners commonly report that it helps with a specific closet or a small bathroom and does little for a whole room, which matches the physics. If you are fighting a humid house, buy a 22-, 35- or 50-pint compressor machine. If you have one stubborn small space, this is a cheap, quiet and sensible add-on."
  },
  {
    "id": "eva-dry-e-333-renewable",
    "name": "Eva-dry E-333 Renewable Mini Dehumidifier",
    "category": "Silica Gel, No Electricity While Drying",
    "badge": "Best No-Power Option for Tiny Spaces",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Uses silica gel beads to absorb moisture, so there is no fan, compressor or noise while it works",
      "Renewable: you plug it in for a few hours to dry the beads and reuse it",
      "Rated by the manufacturer for spaces of roughly 333 cubic feet, such as a small closet or cabinet",
      "No drain hose, bucket or tank to empty",
      "Safe to leave in a gun safe, camera cabinet or an unheated storage space"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only suitable for very small enclosed spaces and needs recharging when the indicator changes colour",
      "It does not replace a dehumidifier for rooms and cannot hold a humidity target"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Not a whole-home solution, but a good answer for a small cabinet, closet or safe. A different tool, at a different price, for a different job.",
    "description": "The Eva-dry E-333 is the odd one out here, and deliberately so. It is a sealed unit filled with silica gel that absorbs moisture from the air with no fan, no compressor and no power draw while it works. When the indicator window changes colour, you plug it into the wall for several hours to dry the beads out and then put it back, which is the renewable part of its name. The manufacturer rates it for spaces of about 333 cubic feet, which is why it suits a small closet, a cabinet, a gun safe or a camera storage box. Because it needs no drain and makes no noise, it is also useful in a space where you would never put a normal dehumidifier. The honest limit is its scope. It cannot hold a humidity setting, it has a small capacity, and it will do nothing for the musty main floor that most readers of this guide are dealing with. Think of it as a piece of storage equipment rather than a home dehumidifier, and it earns its place."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Dehumidifier for Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-dehumidifier-for-home/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size dehumidifier do I need for my house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a typical ordinary house, a 35-pint dehumidifier under the current DOE rating is the safe middle choice. Use a 22-pint model for an apartment or a single mildly damp room and a 50-pint model for very damp or large open spaces. If you are between sizes, choose the larger one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What changed in the 2019 DOE dehumidifier rating?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Department of Energy changed the test conditions to a cooler room, which produces lower pint numbers for the same machine. The standard capacities are now 22, 35 and 50 pints, which roughly replace the older 30, 45 to 50 and 70 pint ratings, so older and newer listings are not directly comparable."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Compressor or desiccant dehumidifier: which is better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A compressor model is better for most heated homes because it is efficient in warm, humid rooms. A desiccant model keeps working in cool spaces such as an unheated garage or sunroom. If your problem room stays below the mid 40s Fahrenheit, a desiccant unit is worth considering."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What humidity should I set my dehumidifier to?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Aim for about 45 to 50 percent in summer. The EPA suggests keeping indoor relative humidity between 30 and 50 percent and below 60 percent to discourage mold. Setting it lower wastes electricity and dries out the air without extra benefit, so use a separate hygrometer to check the real reading."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a dehumidifier all day?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, most are designed for continuous operation with a humidistat that cycles the compressor on and off. Use the hose outlet so you do not have to empty the bucket, and clean the filter every few weeks. If it never switches off, it is probably undersized or the space has a moisture source."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do dehumidifiers use a lot of electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A full-size dehumidifier uses more power than most small appliances, and the draw rises with capacity and with how long it runs. Energy Star certified models use less energy for the same output. The exact cost depends on your electricity rate and how humid your house is, so check our electricity guide for worked examples."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where should I put a dehumidifier in my home?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Put it in the damp room, in open space with about a foot of clearance on every side, away from walls, curtains and furniture. Keep doors and windows closed while it runs, and place it above a drain if you plan to use the hose. A central spot beats a corner for airflow."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Basement Waterproofing",
    tags: [
      "best dehumidifier for home",
      "dehumidifiers for home",
      "35 pint dehumidifier",
      "50 pint dehumidifier",
      "compressor vs desiccant dehumidifier",
      "home dehumidifier"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Dehumidifiers <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">for the Home</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Seven dehumidifiers for ordinary houses, picked by capacity under the 2019 DOE test, drainage options and controls, not by marketing pint numbers.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#frigidaire-ffad3533w1-35-pint"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to Sizing
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-selected" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How These Seven Were Selected</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Our 7 Dehumidifier Picks</a>
          <a href="#pint-rating" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Reading the Pint Rating (2019 DOE)</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. 22, 35 or 50 Pint: Sizing</a>
          <a href="#compressor-desiccant" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Compressor or Desiccant</a>
          <a href="#targets-drainage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Humidity Targets and Drainage</a>
          <a href="#cost-noise" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Running Cost, Noise and Upkeep</a>
          <a href="#not-the-answer" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. When It Is Not the Answer</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A dehumidifier is only as good as its fit to your house. Pick the wrong capacity and it runs constantly and disappoints; pick the right one and a damp, stale home dries out quietly. This guide picks seven by capacity, drainage and controls, and explains how to read the numbers on the box.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-selected" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How These Seven Were Selected
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run these machines through a lab, and this guide does not pretend otherwise. The selection rests on manufacturers' published specifications, how long each model has been on sale, and the pattern of what owners say about living with it. Where we quote a number, it is the figure printed on the label or the spec sheet. That is a weaker claim than saying we measured it, and you should know the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brief was an ordinary house. Think of a main floor that smells stale every August, a laundry room that never quite dries, or a finished room that stays clammy for a week after heavy rain. We were not choosing for a flooded cellar, a nursery or a ducted whole-house system. Those jobs have their own guides, and we link to them instead of repeating them.
          </p>

          <p>
