        <div id="pint-rating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Reading the Pint Rating Under the 2019 DOE Standard
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pint rating on the box is the amount of water a dehumidifier is rated to remove from the air in 24 hours, but the conditions of the test changed in 2019. The Department of Energy moved the test from a warm, humid room to a cooler one, which is closer to the conditions in a real basement or lower level. A machine that carried a bigger number before the change is rated lower now, even though the hardware has not changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The new standard capacities are 22, 35 and 50 pints. Very roughly, those correspond to what used to be sold as 30, 45 to 50, and 70 pints. This is why you will see old and new ratings side by side in listings and why a cheap-looking 70-pint machine can be an older rating of a much smaller unit. If a listing does not say which test it uses, assume the more generous, older one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare pints only within the same test standard</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 50-pint machine rated in 2019 or later is roughly a 70-pint machine from before. Treat any listing that gives only a pint number with suspicion, and check the model number against the manufacturer's page.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            It also helps to know what the number is not. It is not a guarantee of how fast a room will dry, because that depends on the starting humidity, the temperature and how much new moisture arrives each day. A rating of 35 pints describes a controlled test, so treat it as a way to rank machines against each other rather than a forecast for your living room.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more useful lesson is that capacity is a ceiling, not a promise. The pint rating describes a test, and your house, with its leaks, laundry and bathrooms, behaves differently. Our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">full guide to choosing a dehumidifier</a> goes further into why pint ratings alone mislead, and why room conditions matter more than the number on the box.
          </p>
        </div>

