          </div>

          <p>
            The common error is sizing for the whole house when you only need to dry one area. A dehumidifier works on the air in the space where it sits, and it cannot pull moisture through a closed door. If you have one stubborn room, size for that room and accept that the rest of the house will not change much. If you want the entire house treated, that is a ducted job, covered in our guide to <a href="/whole-house-vs-portable-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">whole-house versus portable dehumidifiers</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climate and habits matter as much as square footage. A house in a humid region, a home with several people drying laundry indoors, or a lower level with a bathroom and a washer all produce more moisture than the floor area suggests. In those cases, move up a size from the chart. A dry-climate home with good ventilation can often drop down one.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, round up one size. A unit that is too small runs constantly, never reaches its target and wears faster, while a slightly large one reaches the target and switches off. The extra cost of stepping up is modest, and it is usually the cheapest way to fix a disappointing result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compressor-desiccant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Compressor or Desiccant: What Your Climate Decides
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every machine here is a compressor dehumidifier, which works like a small air conditioner: a cold coil condenses moisture out of the air and the water drains away. They are efficient in warm, humid rooms, which describes most houses in summer. Their weakness is cold. Manufacturers typically specify a minimum operating temperature in the low 40s Fahrenheit, and output falls well before that as the coil begins to frost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Desiccant dehumidifiers use a moisture-absorbing wheel that is regenerated with heat, and they keep working in cool rooms where a compressor would struggle. They draw more power per pint in warm conditions and they give off some warmth, which is a nuisance in summer and a small benefit in a chilly space. For an ordinary heated house, a compressor is the better and cheaper choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your problem room is unheated, such as a garage, a sunroom or a lower level that drops into the 50s in winter, a desiccant model may suit you better. We cover that in more depth in <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">do you need a dehumidifier in winter</a> and in our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-garage/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">garage dehumidifier guide</a>, where cold-weather operation is the main selection factor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature also changes how long a compressor machine takes to do the same work. A rating that holds in a warm room can fall noticeably in a cool one, which is why a unit that dries a main floor in July can seem to struggle in a cool lower level in October. If your space is cool for much of the year, choose the next size up, or read the spec sheet for the stated operating range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two mini picks use yet another method, a thermoelectric plate in the Pro Breeze and silica gel in the Eva-dry. Neither is comparable to a compressor in capacity. They exist for small spaces where a full-size machine is impractical, and they should be bought for that reason alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="targets-drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Humidity Targets, Drainage and Where to Put It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity between about 30 and 50 percent, and below 60 percent to discourage mold. Most households do well setting a dehumidifier to 45 to 50 percent in summer. Setting it lower than that wastes electricity, dries out wood and skin, and makes the machine run constantly without any benefit.
          </p>

          <p>
            A humidistat only helps if you can trust the reading. Built-in sensors can be off by several points, so a cheap standalone meter is worth having. Our guide to the <a href="/best-hygrometer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best hygrometers</a> explains why a separate reading is a sensible check, and a few minutes with one often shows you that the problem room is the one you were not watching.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drainage is where most people give up on a dehumidifier. A bucket is fine for a mini unit, but a full-size machine in a damp season can fill a bucket in a day, and emptying it every morning gets old quickly. Every full-size model here has a hose outlet so that the water runs by gravity to a floor drain, a sink or a standpipe, which is the single feature most likely to keep the machine in use.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have no drain below the machine, you need a pump. A few machines build one in, and ours are not among them, so see <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">our dehumidifiers with pumps</a> and the guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> for the alternatives.
          </p>

          <p>
            For placement, leave a foot or so of clear space around the machine so that air can move through it. Keep it away from walls, curtains and furniture, and close the doors and windows of the room you are treating. A dehumidifier in a room with an open window is fighting the outdoors and will not win.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running Cost, Noise and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            A full-size dehumidifier is not a cheap appliance to run. Power draw rises with capacity, and a machine that runs through a humid summer adds a visible amount to the bill. The honest figure depends on your electricity price and on how hard the machine works, which is why we point you to our worked examples in <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> rather than quoting a single number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Energy Star certification is a reasonable shortcut. Energy Star states that certified dehumidifiers use noticeably less energy than non-certified units, and it publishes a litres-per-kilowatt-hour efficiency threshold. Check each listing for the current status, because it varies by model and year, and treat it as a tiebreaker rather than a reason to buy a machine of the wrong size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is a matter of fan speed and compressor design. Manufacturers often publish a decibel figure measured at the lowest speed, which is flattering, and owners commonly report that a full-size machine on high is louder than expected. If the machine will live near where you sit or sleep, read our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">bedroom dehumidifier guide</a> for the noise-focused picks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upkeep is simple but not optional. Wash the filter every few weeks during heavy use, empty and wipe the bucket to keep it from going slimy, and vacuum the coil once a season. A machine with a clogged filter works harder and dries less, which is how people end up deciding that dehumidifiers do not work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-the-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When a Dehumidifier Is Not the Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier treats the air, not the cause. If water is entering through a wall, a floor crack or a leaking pipe, the machine will remove the moisture you can measure and leave the source untouched. The sign is a bucket that fills much faster than the weather justifies, or damp patches on the wall that return after you dry them.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is a repair job, not a purchase. Start with the exterior: gutters, downspouts and the grade around the foundation. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-keep-a-basement-dry-without-a-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">keeping a basement dry without a dehumidifier</a> covers the fixes that work, and it applies equally to any lower-level room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Another case is confusion with an air purifier. A purifier removes particles and does nothing for humidity, and a dehumidifier does the reverse. They solve different problems, and we explain which one you need in <a href="/air-purifier-vs-dehumidifier/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">air purifier versus dehumidifier</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if the dampness is confined to one wet room, such as a bathroom, a better exhaust fan or a habit of running it for twenty minutes after a shower may do more than any appliance. A dehumidifier is a good tool for chronic, house-wide humidity and a poor substitute for ventilation and repair.
          </p>
        </div>

