            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by the old pint rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 70-pint listing may be an older rating of a smaller machine. Since the 2019 DOE change, the standard capacities are 22, 35 and 50 pints, so compare like with like and check the model number on the manufacturer's page before you trust a big number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing for the whole house</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier dries the room it sits in, and it cannot pull moisture through a closed door. Size for the damp space you actually have, and if you want the whole house treated, look at a ducted system rather than a larger portable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the drain hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full-size machine in a humid summer can fill its bucket in a day. If you plan to empty it by hand, you will probably stop using it within a month. Set it above a drain or use a pump so it can run unattended.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the target too low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The EPA suggests keeping indoor humidity around 30 to 50 percent. Setting a dehumidifier to 30 percent makes it run all day, dries out wood and skin, and adds to the electricity bill without any benefit for mold control.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a leak as a humidity problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the bucket fills faster than the weather justifies, water is probably getting in. A dehumidifier will hide the symptom while the leak continues, so fix gutters, grading or plumbing first and use the machine for what remains.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

