        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a typical ordinary house, a 35-pint dehumidifier under the current DOE rating is the safe middle choice. Use a 22-pint model for an apartment or a single mildly damp room and a 50-pint model for very damp or large open spaces. If you are between sizes, choose the larger one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What changed in the 2019 DOE dehumidifier rating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Department of Energy changed the test conditions to a cooler room, which produces lower pint numbers for the same machine. The standard capacities are now 22, 35 and 50 pints, which roughly replace the older 30, 45 to 50 and 70 pint ratings, so older and newer listings are not directly comparable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Compressor or desiccant dehumidifier: which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A compressor model is better for most heated homes because it is efficient in warm, humid rooms. A desiccant model keeps working in cool spaces such as an unheated garage or sunroom. If your problem room stays below the mid 40s Fahrenheit, a desiccant unit is worth considering.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should I set my dehumidifier to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for about 45 to 50 percent in summer. The EPA suggests keeping indoor relative humidity between 30 and 50 percent and below 60 percent to discourage mold. Setting it lower wastes electricity and dries out the air without extra benefit, so use a separate hygrometer to check the real reading.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a dehumidifier all day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, most are designed for continuous operation with a humidistat that cycles the compressor on and off. Use the hose outlet so you do not have to empty the bucket, and clean the filter every few weeks. If it never switches off, it is probably undersized or the space has a moisture source.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do dehumidifiers use a lot of electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A full-size dehumidifier uses more power than most small appliances, and the draw rises with capacity and with how long it runs. Energy Star certified models use less energy for the same output. The exact cost depends on your electricity rate and how humid your house is, so check our electricity guide for worked examples.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a dehumidifier in my home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Put it in the damp room, in open space with about a foot of clearance on every side, away from walls, curtains and furniture. Keep doors and windows closed while it runs, and place it above a drain if you plan to use the hose. A central spot beats a corner for airflow.
              </p>
            </div>

